WI-rilof made a NAMED binding of an npm workspace sibling resolve. Three
other spellings still called a tier-3 @ws/ stand-in (INV-nuzas), because
the lookup found the sibling's function and the cross-package guard
refused it:
  const b = require('@ws/b'); b.helper()   -> javascript:@ws/b:0-0:helper
  import * as C from '@ws/c'; C.shout()    -> typescript:@ws/c:0-0:shout
  import cDefault from '@ws/c'; cDefault() -> typescript:@ws/c:0-0:default

* A member call on a MODULE OBJECT (import * as, a plain require) takes the
  answer a named binding takes: _disambiguate_by_import, i.e.
  _in_tree_package_candidate, before the guard. namespace_imports also
  holds default imports, whose alias is the default export's VALUE, so
  _extract_namespace_imports now records which aliases are module objects
  and only those take this route; `cd.shout()` on a default import is not
  guessed to be the module's shout.
* A called default binding of an in-tree package is its ENTRY's `export
  default` declaration, whatever the local name (`import x from '@ws/c';
  x()` is cDefault; a relative `import y from './c'; y()` names c's
  default export the same way). The entry is found by Node's rule, now in the new
  core module js_package_entry (package_entry, probe_file), moved verbatim
  from the js_module linker, which imports it back, so the import edge
  and the call agree on the file. A package name two roots declare, a
  self-import, or an entry with no named default declaration names nothing.
  The per-file default-export name lives on _ParsedFile (in memory).
* The item's shape 4: `new b.Engine()` / `b.VERSION` -- a read of an in-tree
  module object's member -- was a module_attr_ref to a @ws/b.Engine
  stand-in beside the real instantiation (relative imports too: ./b.Engine).
  It is now a `references` edge to the in-tree symbol (evidence
  ast_name_read), python's WI-huhum answer to the same shape; references is
  not a taint call edge, so no taint frontier moves. Exact matches only. The
  shared emitter keys edges by module, not alias, so a module the file also
  binds by name or default is left alone (a miss, never a wrong edge).
  Shape 5 (the `<spec>:0-0:module` import stand-ins beside the linker's real
  file edge) is the WI-lipal case and is left for its own change.

